IMPORT prelude

-- Misrepresentation Act 1967 (Singapore) -- the whole operative Act: rescission despite the misrepresentation having become a term or
-- the contract having been performed (s 1), damages for a non-fraudulent misrepresentation and the reversed burden (s 2(1)), damages
-- in lieu of rescission (s 2(2), (3)), clauses excluding liability for misrepresentation (s 3), and the saving for past transactions
-- (s 4). Section 5 (short title) is not encoded.
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/MA1967.txt. The deposit says the revised edition
-- "incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021"; no later amendment
-- is annotated in the text. The deposit's metadata records it as the "Current version as at 01 Oct 2026".
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Chosen for its everyday-life relevance: anyone who buys a flat, a used car, a renovation package or a course on the
-- strength of what the seller said meets this Act when the statement turns out to be false. SCOPED to the whole Act, which is five
-- short sections. Not encoded: what a misrepresentation IS, when rescission is available at common law, the measure of damages, fraud
-- claims at common law, the reasonableness test itself (Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, s 11(1) and its Schedule), and the Civil Law
-- Act 1909 s 5 that s 4 refers to. All of these are inputs here.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules conventions. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Misrepresentation Act 1967`

§§ `1 -- rescission not barred by incorporation or performance`

-- s 1: "Where a person has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him, and -- (a) the misrepresentation
-- has become a term of the contract; or (b) the contract has been performed, or both, then, if otherwise he would be entitled to
-- rescind the contract without alleging fraud, he shall be so entitled, subject to the provisions of this Act, notwithstanding the
-- matters mentioned in paragraphs (a) and (b)."
--
-- The two facts are taken as inputs only to show that they change nothing.
GIVEN `entitled to rescind apart from (a) and (b)` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the misrepresentation became a term` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the contract has been performed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the representee is entitled to rescind under section 1` IF
    `entitled to rescind apart from (a) and (b)`

§§ `2(1) -- damages for a non-fraudulent misrepresentation`

-- s 2(1): "Where a person has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him by another party thereto and as
-- a result thereof he has suffered loss, then, if the person making the misrepresentation would be liable to damages in respect
-- thereof had the misrepresentation been made fraudulently, that person shall be so liable notwithstanding that the misrepresentation
-- was not made fraudulently, unless he proves that he had reasonable ground to believe and did believe up to the time the contract
-- was made that the facts represented were true."
--
-- The representation must come from "another party thereto": a statement by a stranger to the contract is outside s 2(1). The
-- defence is the representor's to PROVE, and needs both reasonable ground and actual belief, lasting until the contract was made.
DECLARE Claim HAS
    `the representee entered the contract after the misrepresentation` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the misrepresentation was made by another party to the contract` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the representee suffered loss as a result` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the representor would be liable in damages had it been fraudulent` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the representor proved reasonable ground to believe the facts true` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the representor proved actual belief up to the time of contracting` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the representor proved the section 2(1) defence` IF
        `the claim`'s `the representor proved reasonable ground to believe the facts true`
    AND `the claim`'s `the representor proved actual belief up to the time of contracting`

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the representor is liable in damages under section 2(1)` IF
        `the claim`'s `the representee entered the contract after the misrepresentation`
    AND `the claim`'s `the misrepresentation was made by another party to the contract`
    AND `the claim`'s `the representee suffered loss as a result`
    AND `the claim`'s `the representor would be liable in damages had it been fraudulent`
    AND (NOT `the representor proved the section 2(1) defence` `the claim`)

§§ `2(2), (3) -- damages in lieu of rescission`

-- s 2(2): where the misrepresentation was made "otherwise than fraudulently" and the representee "would be entitled, by reason of the
-- misrepresentation, to rescind the contract, then, if it is claimed, in any proceedings arising out of the contract, that the
-- contract ought to be or has been rescinded, the court or arbitrator MAY declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu
-- of rescission, if of opinion that it would be equitable to do so, having regard to the nature of the misrepresentation and the loss
-- that would be caused by it if the contract were upheld, as well as to the loss that rescission would cause to the other party."
--
-- The equitable judgment is an input. Unlike s 2(1), s 2(2) does not require the misrepresentation to come from a party, and the
-- representor has no belief defence: s 2(3) "Damages may be awarded against a person under subsection (2) whether or not he is liable
-- to damages under subsection (1)".
GIVEN `the misrepresentation was fraudulent` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the representee is entitled to rescind` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `rescission is claimed in proceedings arising out of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the court or arbitrator thinks it equitable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court or arbitrator may award damages in lieu of rescission` IF
        (NOT `the misrepresentation was fraudulent`)
    AND `the representee is entitled to rescind`
    AND `rescission is claimed in proceedings arising out of the contract`
    AND `the court or arbitrator thinks it equitable`

-- Putting ss 1 and 2(2) together: s 1 entitles the representee to rescind "subject to the provisions of this Act", and s 2(2) lets
-- the court keep the contract alive. Whether the court DOES so is an input (`the court declares the contract subsisting`); it can only
-- do so where s 2(2) allows.
GIVEN `the misrepresentation was fraudulent` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `entitled to rescind apart from (a) and (b)` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the misrepresentation became a term` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the contract has been performed` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the court or arbitrator thinks it equitable` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the court declares the contract subsisting` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the representee ends up with rescission` IF
        `the representee is entitled to rescind under section 1` `entitled to rescind apart from (a) and (b)` `the misrepresentation became a term` `the contract has been performed`
    AND (NOT (    `the court declares the contract subsisting`
              AND `the court or arbitrator may award damages in lieu of rescission` `the misrepresentation was fraudulent` `entitled to rescind apart from (a) and (b)` TRUE `the court or arbitrator thinks it equitable`))

-- s 2(3): "where he is so liable any award under subsection (2) shall be taken into account in assessing his liability under
-- subsection (1)." INFERENCE: "taken into account" is read as a deduction from the s 2(1) assessment, never below zero. The text does
-- not say subtract.
GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the larger of` MEANS IF `a` GREATER THAN `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

GIVEN `damages assessed under section 2(1)` IS A NUMBER, `award already made under section 2(2)` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the further amount payable under section 2(1)` MEANS
    `the larger of` 0 (`damages assessed under section 2(1)` MINUS `award already made under section 2(2)`)

§§ `3 -- clauses excluding liability for misrepresentation`

-- s 3: "If a contract contains a term which would exclude or restrict -- (a) any liability to which a party to a contract may be
-- subject by reason of any misrepresentation made by him before the contract was made; or (b) any remedy available to another party
-- to the contract by reason of such a misrepresentation, that term shall be of no effect except in so far as it satisfies the
-- requirement of reasonableness as stated in section 11(1) of the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, and it is for those claiming that
-- the term satisfies that requirement to show that it does."
--
-- Whether a given clause (an "entire agreement" or "non-reliance" clause, say) excludes liability at all is a question the Act does
-- not answer; the kinds below are ones whose effect is on the face of the clause. "In so far as" allows a term to survive in part;
-- this encoding answers only whether the term has effect at all.
DECLARE Term IS ONE OF
    `a clause excluding all liability for statements made before the contract`
    `a clause capping damages for misrepresentation`
    `a clause taking away the right to rescind for misrepresentation`
    `a clause fixing the delivery date`
    `a clause choosing the governing law`

GIVEN `the term` IS A Term
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the term excludes or restricts misrepresentation liability or remedies` IF
    CONSIDER `the term`
    WHEN `a clause excluding all liability for statements made before the contract` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a clause capping damages for misrepresentation` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a clause taking away the right to rescind for misrepresentation` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the term` IS A Term, `the party relying on it showed it is reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the term has effect` IF
        (NOT `the term excludes or restricts misrepresentation liability or remedies` `the term`)
    OR  `the party relying on it showed it is reasonable`

§§ `4 -- saving for past transactions`

-- s 4: "Nothing in this Act applies in relation to any misrepresentation or contract of sale made before 12 November 1993 except in so
-- far as it was applicable by virtue of section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909 in force before that date."
--
-- Read literally: a misrepresentation made before 12 November 1993 is outside the Act; so is a CONTRACT OF SALE made before that
-- date, even if (improbably) the misrepresentation came later. For any other contract only the date of the misrepresentation is
-- named. Dates are YYYYMMDD numbers. The extent of the Civil Law Act 1909 s 5 carve-out was not read; it is an input.
GIVEN `misrepresentation date` IS A NUMBER,
      `contract date` IS A NUMBER,
      `the contract is a contract of sale` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `applicable by virtue of Civil Law Act section 5` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act applies` IF
        `applicable by virtue of Civil Law Act section 5`
    OR  (    `misrepresentation date` AT LEAST 19931112
         AND (    (NOT `the contract is a contract of sale`)
              OR  `contract date` AT LEAST 19931112))
